An API Gateway is a single entry point for client requests that routes them to the appropriate backend service.
It is especially common in microservices architecture.
Suppose you have:
User Service
Order Service
Payment Service
Product Service

Without an API Gateway, the client may need to communicate directly with different services:
                 ┌──→ User Service
Client ──────────┼──→ Order Service
                 ├──→ Payment Service
                 └──→ Product Service

With an API Gateway:
                       ┌──→ User Service
                       │
Client → API Gateway ──┼──→ Order Service
                       │
                       ├──→ Payment Service
                       │
                       └──→ Product Service

The client only needs to know about the API Gateway.
Example
Client sends:
GET /users/101

API Gateway routes it to:
User Service

Another request:
POST /orders

goes to:
Order Service

So:
/users/*     → User Service
/orders/*    → Order Service
/payments/*  → Payment Service
/products/*  → Product Service

This is called request routing.
But API Gateway does more than routing
It can handle common concerns before requests reach individual services:
                    Client
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌────────────────┐
              │  API Gateway   │
              │                │
              │ Authentication │
              │ Rate Limiting  │
              │ Routing        │
              │ Logging        │
              │ SSL/TLS        │
              │ API Versioning │
              └────────────────┘
                       │
             ┌─────────┼─────────┐
             ▼         ▼         ▼
           User      Order     Payment
          Service   Service    Service

Important responsibilities include:
- Request routing
- Authentication / authorization enforcement
- Rate limiting
- Logging and monitoring
- TLS termination
- Request/response transformation
- API versioning
- Sometimes response aggregation
Why is it useful?
Imagine you have 50 microservices.
Without a gateway:
Client needs knowledge of:

user-service...
order-service...
payment-service...
inventory-service...
notification-service...
...

That's messy and exposes backend service structure.
Instead:
Client
   ↓
api.example.com
   ↓
API Gateway
   ↓
50 internal services

The gateway hides much of the internal architecture from the client.
API Gateway vs Load Balancer
This is a common confusion.
A Load Balancer primarily distributes traffic among multiple instances of a service:
                Load Balancer
                /     |     \
               ↓      ↓      ↓
           Server1 Server2 Server3

An API Gateway primarily manages and routes API requests to different backend services:
                    API Gateway
                  /      |       \
                 ↓       ↓        ↓
              User     Order    Payment
             Service  Service   Service

They can exist together:
Client
   ↓
API Gateway
   ↓
Load Balancer
   ↓
┌──────┬──────┬──────┐
▼      ▼      ▼
S1     S2     S3

In real systems, their features can overlap, so don't memorize that they are always separate physical components.
Interview definition
An API Gateway is a single entry point between clients and backend services that routes API requests and can handle cross-cutting concerns such as authentication, rate limiting, logging, and request transformation.

Easy memory:
Load Balancer → “Which instance should handle this traffic?”
API Gateway → “Which service/API should this request go to, and what API-level policies should be applied?”